.TH CGSOLITAIRE 6 "November 2026" "cggames 1.4.0"
.SH NAME
cgsolitaire \- the classic Solitaire card game
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cgsolitaire
.RI [ "toolkit options" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cgsolitaire
plays Klondike, the patience the classic desktop Solitaire played.
Seven columns are dealt, the last card of each face up, and the rest of
the pack is the stock.
Build the four suits up from their aces on the foundations at the top
right to win.
.PP
In the columns, a card goes on one a rank higher of the other colour,
and an empty column takes only a king.
Face up cards can be moved together, from any of them down.
Clicking on the stock turns one card, or three, onto the waste beside
it, whose top card can be played; when the stock is empty, clicking on
it turns the waste over to be the stock again.
An O on the empty stock means it can be turned again; an X means it
can't.
Clicking on a face down card at the foot of a column turns it up.
.PP
Cards are dragged with the left button, or clicked on and then put
down with a second click where they are to go.
A double click sends a card to its foundation; the right button sends
every card that can go.
Escape lets go of cards picked up.
.B Undo
takes back the last move.
.PP
The game can be played from the keyboard, as Windows Solitaire could.
The first key shows a yellow ring round the place it is on.
Tab goes from the deck to the foundations to the columns, and Shift
with Tab back.
Left and Right go along a row; Down goes from the top row to the
column under it, and Up from a column to the top row.
In a column, Up and Down choose the face up card a run is taken from,
the ring going round all the cards that would move.
Space or Enter turns the stock, turns up a column's face down card, or
picks up the cards in the ring, shown in reverse colours; pressed again
where they are to go, it puts them down.
Escape lets go of them, and using the mouse hides the ring.
.PP
When the game is won, the cards bounce off the foundations; a click or
a key stops them.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B Draw
One or three cards turned from the stock at a time.
.TP
.B Scoring
Standard scoring gives 5 points for a card from the waste to a column,
10 for one to a foundation from the waste or a column, and 5 for
turning up a card in a column, and takes off 15 for a card brought back
from a foundation.
Each time the waste is turned over costs 100 points drawing one, and
20 drawing three from the fourth time.
The score never goes below 0.
.IP
Vegas scoring costs $52 a game and pays $5 for each card on the
foundations.
The stock can be gone through only once drawing one, three times
drawing three.
With
.B Cumulative score
each game's dollars carry on to the next while the program runs.
.IP
None keeps no score.
.TP
.B Timed game
Shows the time.
With standard scoring, a timed game loses 2 points every 10 seconds,
and a game won in 30 seconds or more gets a bonus of 700000 divided by
its seconds.
The clock starts with the first move and stops while the window is
minimized.
.TP
.B Status bar
Shows the score and the time under the table.
.TP
.B Outline dragging
Drags the edge of the cards, leaving them in place until they are put
down.
.PP
Changing the draw, the scoring or the timing deals a new game.
.PP
The cards are drawn as large as the window lets the table be, larger
or smaller with it, down to three quarters of their usual size; a
window wider than the table needs spreads the piles apart, as on
Windows.
.B Deck
picks the design on the backs of the cards.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cggames/cgsolitaire.conf
The options and the card back.
.PP
Without the variable,
.I ~/.config
is used.
.SH AUTHORS
cgsolitaire was written for cggames.
Its card faces are drawn from a public domain deck, in SVG; the backs
are drawn by the program.
